  </section>  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>The Science Behind One-Rep Max Prediction</h2><p>
  Your one-rep max (1RM) is the heaviest weight you can successfully
  complete for one repetition of a specific exercise using a defined
  technique and range of motion. Testing a true 1RM can be demanding,
  so many lifters use a submaximal set to estimate it instead.
</p>

<p>
  This calculator uses four established repetition-based prediction
  equations: Epley, Brzycki, Lander, and Lombardi. These equations
  produce estimates rather than direct measurements, and different
  formulas can give different results for the same set.
</p>

  </article>  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>How to Use This 1RM Calculator</h2><p>
  Enter the weight you lifted and the number of completed repetitions.
  Choose pounds (lb) or kilograms (kg), select a formula, and calculate
  your estimated one-rep max.
</p>

<p>
  For a useful starting estimate, use a clean set performed with
  consistent technique. Research on repetition-based prediction shows
  that accuracy can vary with the number of repetitions, exercise,
  population, and prediction equation, so the result should not be
  treated as a guaranteed maximum.
</p>

<p>
  After calculating your 1RM, use our
  <a href="/tools/strength/1rm-percentage-calculator">
    1RM Percentage Calculator
  </a>
  to convert it into training loads, or use the
  <a href="/tools/strength/rep-max-calculator">
    Rep Max Calculator
  </a>
  to estimate a different target repetition weight.
</p>

  </article>  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>1RM Percentage Chart &amp; Training Zones</h2><p>
  A 1RM percentage chart helps translate your estimated maximum into
  training loads. Strength programs commonly use percentages of 1RM to
  describe training intensity, but the number of repetitions a person
  can perform at a given percentage varies between exercises and
  individuals.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>90%–100%:</strong>
    Very heavy loading commonly associated with low-repetition
    strength work and maximal-strength practice.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>80%–85%:</strong>
    Moderate-to-heavy loading commonly used for strength-focused and
    mixed strength/hypertrophy training.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>70%–75%:</strong>
    Moderate loading often used for higher-volume strength and
    hypertrophy work.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>60%–65%:</strong>
    Lighter loading that can be useful for higher-repetition work,
    technique practice, warm-ups, or other program-specific purposes.
  </li>
</ul>

<p>
  These are general reference ranges, not fixed rules. Actual training
  loads should reflect the exercise, program, training experience,
  technique, fatigue, and recovery.
</p>

<p>
  The NSCA describes percentage-of-1RM loading as one method for
  prescribing resistance, while also noting that repetition capacity
  at a given percentage can vary between exercises and individuals.
</p>

  </article>  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>The 4 Core 1RM Formulas Explained</h2><p>
  Each equation models the relationship between a submaximal weight and
  the number of repetitions completed. Because the mathematical models
  differ, the estimated 1RM can change depending on which formula you
  select.
</p>

<h3>1. Epley Formula</h3>

<p>
  <code>1RM = Weight × (1 + Reps / 30)</code>
</p>

<p>
  The Epley equation is a commonly used repetition-based prediction
  model and is frequently included in comparisons of 1RM equations.
</p>

<h3>2. Brzycki Formula</h3>

<p>
  <code>1RM = Weight × 36 / (37 − Reps)</code>
</p>

<p>
  The Brzycki equation is another widely used prediction model. Its
  denominator reaches zero at 37 repetitions, so this calculator blocks
  the formula at 37 reps and above.
</p>

<h3>3. Lander Formula</h3>

<p>
  <code>1RM = (100 × Weight) / (101.3 − 2.67123 × Reps)</code>
</p>

<p>
  The Lander equation uses a denominator that decreases as repetitions
  increase. This calculator blocks inputs once that denominator is no
  longer positive, preventing invalid or non-physical results.
</p>

<h3>4. Lombardi Formula</h3>

<p>
  <code>1RM = Weight × Reps^0.10</code>
</p>

<p>
  Lombardi uses a non-linear power relationship between repetitions and
  estimated maximum strength. Research has evaluated Lombardi alongside
  other repetition-based equations in specific populations, but no
  single equation should be assumed to be universally most accurate.
</p>

  </article>  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Why Can 1RM Estimates Differ Between Exercises?</h2><p>
  A prediction equation does not know your exercise technique, range of
  motion, training history, fatigue profile, or individual strength
  curve. The same person can therefore receive different-quality
  estimates for different lifts.
</p>

<p>
  Research on bench press has found useful prediction from
  submaximal repetition performance, while also showing that prediction
  behavior can change as repetitions increase. One study of 220 men
  found stronger prediction performance when sets were at or below
  10 repetitions and recommended caution when using higher-repetition
  sets to estimate 1RM.
</p>

<p>
  Research also shows that prediction accuracy can differ between
  exercises and testing methods. Therefore, use your estimated 1RM as
  a planning reference rather than assuming it is the exact weight you
  can lift on a particular day.
</p>

  </article>  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Should the Barbell Weight Be Included?</h2><p>
  Yes. If you are calculating a barbell lift, enter the total load you
  actually lifted, including the bar and all plates.
</p>

<p>
  A 45 lb (20 kg) Olympic-style bar is common in many gyms, but barbell
  weights vary by bar type, manufacturer, and training environment.
  Check the actual bar weight rather than assuming every bar has the
  same unloaded weight.
</p>

  </article>  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>When Should You Use an Estimated 1RM?</h2><p>
  An estimated 1RM is useful when you want a practical strength reference
  without performing a true maximal single. It can help with
  percentage-based programming, tracking changes in strength, and
  choosing starting loads.
</p>

<p>
  A direct 1RM test is a different measurement: it tests the maximum
  load you can successfully complete under the test conditions. It
  requires appropriate preparation, technique, equipment, and judgment,
  particularly when heavy free-weight lifts are involved.
</p>

<p>
  If you prefer to work from a conservative training max, use our
  <a href="/tools/training/training-max-calculator">
    Training Max Calculator
  </a>
  after estimating your 1RM.
</p>

  </article>  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Sources &amp; References</h2><p>
  The equations and concepts discussed on this page come from established
  resistance-training and 1RM-prediction research. The cited studies
  evaluate prediction equations in specific populations and exercises,
  so their results should not be interpreted as proof that one formula
  is universally best for every lifter.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    Mayhew JL, Prinster JL, Ware JS, Zimmer DL, Arabas JR, Bemben MG.
    <a
      href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7500624/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Muscular endurance repetitions to predict bench press strength in
      men of different training levels
    </a>.
    <em>Journal of Sports Medicine and Physical Fitness</em>. 1995.
  </li>

  <li>
    LeSuer DA, McCormick JH, Mayhew JL, Wasserstein RL, Arnold MD.
    <a
      href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16937972/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Prediction of one repetition maximum strength from multiple
      repetition maximum testing and anthropometry
    </a>.
  </li>

  <li>
    <a
      href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28485383/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Validity of one-repetition maximum predictive equations
    </a>
    provides additional cross-validation evidence for established
    prediction equations, including Lombardi, in a specific population
    and exercise.
  </li>

  <li>
    National Strength and Conditioning Association.
    <a
      href="https://www.nsca.com/education/articles/kinetic-select/intensity-or-resistance/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Intensity or Resistance
    </a>.
    Overview of RM targets and percentage-of-1RM loading.
  </li>

  <li>
    Stone MH, Hornsby G, Reed J, Stone M, Duca M, Mizuguchi S, Pierce K.
    <a
      href="https://www.nsca.com/education/articles/nsca-coach/using-intensity-based-on-sets-and-repetitions-over-50-years-of-experience-a-brief-overview-of-load-setting-and-programming-strategy/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Using Intensity Based on Sets and Repetitions
    </a>.
    <em>NSCA Coach</em>. 2025.
  </li>
</ul>

<p>
  RepMaths does not claim that any one formula is universally the most
  accurate. Comparing multiple estimates can provide useful context,
  especially when the input set is outside the range where a particular
  equation has been studied.
</p>

  </article>  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="faq-item">
  <h3>What is a 1RM calculator?</h3>
  <p>
    A 1RM calculator estimates the maximum weight you could lift for one
    repetition from a lighter weight and completed repetition count. It
    uses a mathematical prediction equation rather than directly testing
    your maximum.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
  <h3>Which 1RM formula is the most accurate?</h3>
  <p>
    No formula is guaranteed to be the most accurate for every lifter,
    exercise, or rep range. Epley, Brzycki, Lander, and Lombardi can
    produce different estimates, so it is useful to treat the result as
    an estimate rather than a guaranteed maximum.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
  <h3>Can I calculate my 1RM using more than 10 reps?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes, but prediction uncertainty can increase as the number of
    repetitions rises, and different equations may diverge more.
    Research on bench press has found useful prediction from
    submaximal sets while supporting caution with higher-repetition
    inputs.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
  <h3>Why can my 1RM estimate differ between bench press and squat?</h3>
  <p>
    1RM prediction accuracy can vary by exercise because different lifts
    involve different movement patterns, muscle groups, technique
    demands, and fatigue characteristics. A formula that gives a useful
    estimate for one lift is not automatically equally accurate for
    another.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
  <h3>Should I include the barbell weight in my 1RM calculation?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. Enter the total load you lifted, including the bar and plates.
    Because barbell weights vary, use the actual weight of the bar you
    trained with rather than assuming a universal bar weight.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
  <h3>What is a 1RM percentage chart used for?</h3>
  <p>
    A 1RM percentage chart converts an estimated maximum into training
    loads. Coaches and lifters commonly use percentages of 1RM to
    describe training intensity, but the exact repetitions possible at
    a percentage vary by person, exercise, and program.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
  <h3>Does this formula work the same for men and women?</h3>
  <p>
    These general prediction equations can be used with both men and
    women. However, prediction accuracy can vary with the exercise,
    population, training experience, repetition range, and testing
    conditions.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
  <h3>Is estimating a 1RM better than testing a true 1-rep max?</h3>
  <p>
    An estimate can be a practical alternative when you do not want to
    perform a true maximal single. A direct 1RM test measures actual
    performance under the test conditions, but it involves heavier
    loading and should be approached with appropriate technique,
    preparation, equipment, and safety considerations.
  </p>
</div>

  </article>  <RelatedTools currentToolId="1rm-calculator" />  <script>
    let unit = "lb";
    let formula = "epley";

    const unitLb = document.getElementById("unit-lb");
    const unitKg = document.getElementById("unit-kg");

    const formEpley = document.getElementById("formula-epley");
    const formBrzycki = document.getElementById("formula-brzycki");
    const formLander = document.getElementById("formula-lander");
    const formLombardi = document.getElementById("formula-lombardi");

    const weightInput =
      document.getElementById("weight") as HTMLInputElement | null;

    const repsInput =
      document.getElementById("reps") as HTMLInputElement | null;

    const calcBtn = document.getElementById("calc-btn");
    const result1rm = document.getElementById("result-1rm");
    const resultLabel = document.getElementById("result-label");
    const resultTableBody =
      document.getElementById("result-table-body");

    const resultBox =
      document.getElementById("result-box");

    const inputError =
      document.getElementById("input-error");

    if (unitLb) {
      unitLb.addEventListener("click", () => setUnit("lb"));
    }

    if (unitKg) {
      unitKg.addEventListener("click", () => setUnit("kg"));
    }

    if (formEpley) {
      formEpley.addEventListener(
        "click",
        () => setFormula("epley")
      );
    }

    if (formBrzycki) {
      formBrzycki.addEventListener(
        "click",
        () => setFormula("brzycki")
      );
    }

    if (formLander) {
      formLander.addEventListener(
        "click",
        () => setFormula("lander")
      );
    }

    if (formLombardi) {
      formLombardi.addEventListener(
        "click",
        () => setFormula("lombardi")
      );
    }

    function setUnit(newUnit: string) {
      if (newUnit === unit) return;

      if (weightInput) {
        const currentVal =
          Number(weightInput.value);

        if (
          Number.isFinite(currentVal) &&
          currentVal > 0
        ) {
          weightInput.value =
            newUnit === "kg"
              ? (currentVal / 2.20462).toFixed(1)
              : (currentVal * 2.20462).toFixed(1);
        }
      }

      unit = newUnit;

      if (unitLb) {
        unitLb.classList.toggle(
          "active",
          unit === "lb"
        );

        unitLb.setAttribute(
          "aria-pressed",
          String(unit === "lb")
        );
      }

      if (unitKg) {
        unitKg.classList.toggle(
          "active",
          unit === "kg"
        );

        unitKg.setAttribute(
          "aria-pressed",
          String(unit === "kg")
        );
      }

      clearError();
    }

    function setFormula(f: string) {
      formula = f;

      const map: Record<
        string,
        HTMLElement | null
      > = {
        epley: formEpley,
        brzycki: formBrzycki,
        lander: formLander,
        lombardi: formLombardi
      };

      for (const key in map) {
        const el = map[key];

        if (el) {
          el.classList.toggle(
            "active",
            f === key
          );

          el.setAttribute(
            "aria-pressed",
            String(f === key)
          );
        }
      }

      clearError();
    }

    function formulaLabel(f: string) {
      if (f === "epley") return "Epley";
      if (f === "brzycki") return "Brzycki";
      if (f === "lander") return "Lander";
      return "Lombardi";
    }

    function clearError() {
      if (inputError) {
        inputError.textContent = "";
        inputError.style.display = "none";
      }
    }

    function showError(message: string) {
      if (inputError) {
        inputError.textContent = message;
        inputError.style.display = "block";
      }

      if (resultBox) {
        resultBox.classList.remove("show");
      }

      if (result1rm) {
        result1rm.textContent = "–";
      }

      if (resultLabel) {
        resultLabel.textContent =
          "Estimated One-Rep Max";
      }

      if (resultTableBody) {
        resultTableBody.innerHTML = "";
      }
    }

    function calculateOneRM(
      weight: number,
      reps: number
    ): number {
      if (reps === 1) {
        return weight;
      }

      if (formula === "epley") {
        return weight * (1 + reps / 30);
      }

      if (formula === "brzycki") {
        return (
          weight * 36 / (37 - reps)
        );
      }

      if (formula === "lander") {
        return (
          100 * weight
        ) / (
          101.3 - (2.67123 * reps)
        );
      }

      return (
        weight * Math.pow(reps, 0.10)
      );
    }

    if (calcBtn) {
      calcBtn.addEventListener(
        "click",
        () => {
          clearError();

          if (!weightInput || !repsInput) {
            return;
          }

          const weight =
            Number(weightInput.value);

          const reps =
            Number(repsInput.value);

          if (
            !Number.isFinite(weight) ||
            weight <= 0
          ) {
            showError(
              "Please enter a valid weight greater than zero."
            );
            return;
          }

          if (
            !Number.isFinite(reps) ||
            !Number.isInteger(reps) ||
            reps < 1 ||
            reps > 100
          ) {
            showError(
              "Please enter a whole-number rep count from 1 to 100."
            );
            return;
          }

          if (
            formula === "brzycki" &&
            reps >= 37
          ) {
            showError(
              "The Brzycki formula is not defined for 37 or more reps. Try Epley or Lombardi for this rep count."
            );
            return;
          }

          if (formula === "lander") {
            const denominator =
              101.3 -
              (2.67123 * reps);

            if (denominator <= 0) {
              showError(
                "The Lander formula is not valid at this rep count. Try Epley or Lombardi for this rep count."
              );
              return;
            }
          }

          const oneRM =
            calculateOneRM(
              weight,
              reps
            );

          if (
            !Number.isFinite(oneRM) ||
            oneRM <= 0
          ) {
            showError(
              "Unable to calculate a valid 1RM from these inputs."
            );
            return;
          }

          const rounded =
            Math.round(
              oneRM * 10
            ) / 10;

          if (result1rm) {
            result1rm.textContent =
              `${rounded} ${unit}`;
          }

          if (resultLabel) {
            resultLabel.textContent =
              `Estimated One-Rep Max (${formulaLabel(formula)} formula)`;
          }

          const percentages =
            [95, 90, 85, 80, 75, 70, 65, 60];

          const rows =
            percentages
              .map((p) => {
                const val =
                  Math.round(
                    rounded *
                    (p / 100) *
                    10
                  ) / 10;

                return `
                  <tr>
                    <td>${p}%</td>
                    <td>${val} ${unit}</td>
                  </tr>
                `;
              })
              .join("");

          if (resultTableBody) {
            resultTableBody.innerHTML =
              rows;
          }

          if (resultBox) {
            resultBox.classList.add(
              "show"
            );
          }
        }
      );
    }
  </script>
</Base>
